.TH HMAP_TRUNCATE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
hmap_truncate \- Clear all elements from a hash map

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/hmap_internal.h>

.IB int
.IB hmap_truncate(HMAP_DB** hmap_db);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBhmap_truncate\fR function clears all elements from a hash map. It takes a double pointer to the hash map database as an argument.

The function takes the following parameter:
.IB HMAP_DB**
.IB hmap_db
A double pointer to the hash map database.

The function returns 0 upon success. If an error occurs during the truncation process, the function may return an error code.

The \fBhmap_truncate\fR function is intended to be used to remove all elements from a hash map, effectively resetting it to an empty state.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBhmap_truncate\fR function to clear all elements from a hash map:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/hmap_internal.h>

int main() {
    HMAP_DB* hmap_db = 0;

    // Initialize the hash map database
    hmap_init(&hmap_db);

    // Set some elements in the hash map
    // ...

    // Clear all elements from the hash map
    if (hmap_truncate(&hmap_db) != 0) {
        printf("Failed to truncate the hash map\n");
    } else {
        printf("Hash map truncated successfully\n");
    }

    // Free the hash map database
    hmap_destroy(&hmap_db);

    return 0;
}
.nef